Concurrent Write & Read Lock (FlightServerError)

PROBLEM DESCRIPTION:
During feature group execution, running fg.read() directly after or inside the same script as fg.insert() results in a server-side Arrow Flight query crash:

pyarrow._flight.FlightServerError: Set changed size during iteration. Detail: Failed
FeatureStoreException: Could not read data using Hopsworks Query Service.

ROOT CAUSE:
fg.insert() triggers an asynchronous backend materialization job on Apache Spark/Flink to write Parquet files and index HUDI metadata. Calling the offline DuckDB query engine (fg.read()) while the backend job is actively mutating the metadata causes internal thread-locking errors in the shared serverless cluster engine.

SOLUTION:

Approach A: Separate Execution Contexts
Keep ingestion (ingest.py) and reading/verification (eda.py) in completely separate scripts. Run the verification script only after the backend job finishes.

Approach B: Bypass Arrow Flight via the Online Store
Read directly from the MySQL/RonDB online storage layer to avoid DuckDB read-locks during data verification:

Reads directly from the online storage layer without hitting DuckDB
df_verified = fg.read(online=True)
print(f"Verified {df_verified.shape[0]} rows.")

Auto-Restarting Ingestion Jobs

PROBLEM DESCRIPTION:
Stopping a failed or hanging materialization job from the Hopsworks UI results in the job immediately restarting itself on the cluster.

ROOT CAUSE:
The local Python process running ingest_features.py (or a retrying loop in an active Jupyter notebook kernel) remains alive. The client SDK continuously retries failed REST API requests, sending job-creation commands back to the Hopsworks API whenever the remote job is stopped.

SOLUTION:

Kill Local Client Processes: Terminate the local terminal execution, or restart the Jupyter notebook kernel (Ctrl + C or Kernel -> Restart).

Stop and Delete Job: Navigate to Hopsworks UI -> Jobs, click Stop on the running job, then click Delete to purge the backend queue.

Re-ingest Without Process Blocking: Drop the corrupted feature group version and re-ingest to a clean version without setting client-side blocking flags (wait_for_job=True).

DNS Resolution Failure (NameResolutionError / gaierror)

PROBLEM DESCRIPTION:
Attempting to connect to Hopsworks via hopsworks.login() raises a network resolution exception:

gaierror: Name or service not known: eu-west.cloud.hopsworks.ai
ConnectionError: Failed to resolve 'eu-west.cloud.hopsworks.ai'

ROOT CAUSE:
The client environment contains corrupted cached credentials pointing to an unreachable region endpoint, or local DNS settings fail to resolve the cloud host domain.

SOLUTION:

Explicit Host Parameter: Pass the global SaaS domain directly into the login function:

import hopsworks

project = hopsworks.login(
host="c.app.hopsworks.ai",
port=443
)

Clear Cached Credentials: Purge invalid cached API configs stored in your operating system user directory:

Windows: Delete all files inside %USERPROFILE%.hopsworks\

Linux/macOS: Delete all files inside ~/.hopsworks/

Flush Network Cache (Windows): Run 'ipconfig /flushdns' in an elevated terminal to clear stale host cache.